From u8c8975f95cb8f4df at groups.google.com  Wed Jun  8 22:02:20 2022
From: u8c8975f95cb8f4df at groups.google.com (Teunis Jansen)
Date: Wed, 08 Jun 2022 22:02:20 +0000
Subject: inla.posterior.sample.eval() with factors, smoothers and SRF
Message-ID: <RA3DL9l1WYw.yu3CcunvAgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/RA3DL9l1WYw/m/yu3CcunvAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

 Hi All

We have fitted a Negbin model to our fish survey data. Next we predict for certain combinations of covariates and a specific location. We use posterior sampling with inla.posterior.sample() and inla.posterior.sample.eval().

Some background info: We have one survey with many stations per year (year as factor), depth information for each station (depth as smoothed covariate) and there is spatially dependency, but with a different spatial pattern each year, so we included one SRF per year.

We have tested this in a small example with only linear covariates and it worked fine.
So we moved on and called inla.posterior.sample() with our model fit, then passed a data.frame to fun() with the covariates we wanted to predict for. Finally used fun() as an argument passed to inla.posterior.sample.eval(). However, in our analysis we have a factorial parameter (year), a smoother (depth) and the position (for the SRF’s).

How do we pass this into inla.posterior.sample.eval() via the fun()-argument?

Below is the relevant parts of our code boiled down to three years (the factor) and one smoother (Depth_m) and the SRFs.

The question is thus threefold: How to pass

i)                a factor ?

ii)              a smoother ?

iii)             lat-lon ?

Answers to any of these would be highly appreciated.

Kind regards

Søren and Teunis

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

#First we make the depth smoother like this (the name-stuff is just for multiple smoothers and can be ignored here):

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

f(Depth) = XDepth * beta

Lego.Depth_m <- smoothCon(s(Depth_m, bs = "cr", k = 3, fx = TRUE),

                          data = dfModelData,

                          absorb.cons = TRUE)[[1]]

XDepth_m <- Lego.Depth_m$X  #X matrix for the Depth smoother

XDepth_m.df <- data.frame(XDepth_m)  #' It is needed as a data frame for the lincomb.

names(XDepth_m.df) <- paste0("XDepth_m", 1:ncol(XDepth_m))  #' Add column names to the data frame.

lcs.Depth_m <- inla.make.lincombs(XDepth_m.df)  #' Define the linear combination XDepth * beta.

names(lcs.Depth_m) <- paste(names(lcs.Depth_m), "Depth_m", sep = "") #Rename

lcs <- c(lcs.Depth_m) #Select the smoothers that should be used.

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

#Then we make a mesh, a stack, etc, (code excluded)

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

#And then the formula

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

formulaGAM_YearlySRF <- formula(ResponseVar ~ -1 + Intercept +

 fYear2016 + fYear2017 + #The three years 2015-2017 as factors

                XDepth_m + # The depth smoother

                f(w, model = spde.holes, replicate = w.repl))      #The SRFs

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Then we fit the model:

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fitNegBin_GAM_YearlySRF <- inla(formulaGAM_YearlySRF,

                                lincomb = lcs,

         family = "nbinomial",

                                data = inla.stack.data(StackHoles, spde = spde.holes),

                                control.compute = list(dic = TRUE, waic = TRUE, config=TRUE),

                                control.predictor = list(A = inla.stack.A(StackHoles)))

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# And finally the prediction part of the code where we seek advice and help for how to code it:

#We have already made a prediction data.frame called dfDataPredict with combinations of Years, Depths and spatial positions.

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

#Then we sample:

INLA_Samples_fitNegBin_GAM_YearlySRF = inla.posterior.sample(1000, fitNegBin_GAM_YearlySRF)

#Define fun() – this is NOT working

fun = function(dfPD) {

 return (Intercept + fYear2016 + fYear2017 + XDepth_m

+ f(w, model = spde.holes, replicate = w.repl))

 }

# and get the predictions

lstPredictions = inla.posterior.sample.eval(fun, INLA_Samples_fitNegBin_GAM_YearlySRF, dfPD=dfPredictionData)

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

#End

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

