the eval-function can take any arguments (the '...' in the inla.posterior.sample.eval-function), but you need to bear in mind that with factor covariates, then the factors are already expanded, hence your

XDepth_m

will be expanded into

XDepth_m1 0.625 0.111 0.407 0.625 0.844 0.624 0
XDepth_m2 -11.738 0.676 -13.095 -11.728 -10.439 -11.707 0

as you see in the summary

hence you need to give values of XDepth_m1 and XDepth_m2, like

inla.posterior.sample.eval(fun, samples, XDepth_m1=1, XDepth_m2=2)

and then

fun = function(XDepth_m1, XDepth_m2) {
...
}

of course ..._m1 can be a vector, you just need to deal with that in 'fun'.

you can also pass any other object, like a function

> r=inla(y~1,data = data.frame(y=1:10), control.compute=list(config=TRUE))
> xx=inla.posterior.sample(10, r)
> fun=function(func) { return (func(Intercept)) }
> inla.posterior.sample.eval(fun, xx, func=sin)
sample:1 sample:2 sample:3 sample:4 sample:5
fun[1] -0.5822404575 -0.1498477498 0.7358115759 -0.7724250213 -0.8088427364
sample:6 sample:7 sample:8 sample:9 sample:10
fun[1] -0.1295832055 0.5825358862 -0.6720165809 -0.5416104681 -0.9906089285
>

for the lat-lon, you can make the 'A' matrix for those prediction points before you call, like

A= inla.spde.make.A(mesh=mesh, loc=matrix(your.lat.lon))

and then in fun, add

fun = function(XDepth_m1, XDepth_m2,A) {
}

and then the lat-lon will be

A %*% w

as 'w' is your spde index.

of'course, please check that you get it right....

hope this helps

H

